      //                                  leafCount = 0 = inner node.
      //                texel 1 (.xyzw) = (bbMax.x, bbMax.y, bbMax.z, secondData)
      //                                  for inner: secondData = right child node index
      //                                  for leaf:  secondData = first triangle index
      //              Left child of an inner node is implicitly at nodeIdx + 1.
      //              Capacity must be >= maxNodes * 8. A safe upper bound is
      //              4 * triCount nodes (worst case for a max-leaf-size of 4).
      //
      //   triData:   flat float array, triangles are 12 floats each
      //              (3 RGBA32F texels per triangle, .w channels unused).
      //              Layout per triangle:
      //                texel 0 (.xyzw) = (v0.x, v0.y, v0.z, 0)
      //                texel 1 (.xyzw) = (v1.x, v1.y, v1.z, 0)
      //                texel 2 (.xyzw) = (v2.x, v2.y, v2.z, 0)
      //              The triangles are reordered by the builder so that
      //              triData[i] is the triangle referenced by leaf nodes,
      //              which is why the leaf stores "first triangle index"
      //              (a contiguous range, not a list).
      //              Capacity must be >= triCount * 12.
      //
      //   resultOut: flat int array of length >= 3, populated with
      //              [actualNodeCount, actualTriCount, maxDepthReached].
      //
      // Builder strategy: top-down median split along the longest axis.
      // Leaves contain up to BVH_LEAF_SIZE triangles (4). Simple, robust,
      // and produces good trees in O(N log N) time.
      //
      // SIGNATURE:
      //   __bvhBuild(verts, vertStride, vertCount, indices, triCount,
      //              nodeOut, triOut, resOut)   -- 8 args
      //
      // PARAMETERS:
      //   verts:      flat float array. Vertex positions are read at
      //               offsets vertStride*0, vertStride*1, ... (3 floats
      //               per position).
      //   vertStride: number of floats between consecutive vertex
      //               positions. Use 3 for tightly-packed [x,y,z, x,y,z, ...]
      //               or 8 for the OBJ-interleaved [x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, u,v]
      //               returned by __objVerts. Other strides also fine.
      //   vertCount:  number of vertices in `verts`.
      //   indices:    EITHER a Basil int array of length >= 3*triCount,
      //               with each consecutive triple naming the 3 vertices
      //               of a triangle, OR a non-array value (0, null) which
      //               means "implicit sequential" -- triangle t uses
      //               vertices 3t, 3t+1, 3t+2. Use the implicit form
      //               for triangle-soup inputs (e.g. data straight out of
      //               __objVerts) where every triangle has its own copies.
      //   triCount:   number of triangles.
      //   nodeOut:    output array for packed BVH nodes (8 floats each).
